WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.840
$1298 bought the 1977 Apple II even though its chips came from catalogs. This is First Principles Daily, Episode 137. After those design choices, a First Principle spotlight on the material floor, then the hobby kit market they replaced, and what the remaining gaps still left open. Steve Wozniak built the machine from parts a distributor would sell to anyone. A MOS technology 6502, dynamic RAM, and ordinary discrete parts.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.620
He did not stop at a bare processor board.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.560
Color video went on to the motherboard, so a television or a monitor became the display

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.360
instead of a separate video terminal.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:44.020
Eight expansion slots led an owner ad memory or peripherals later instead of buying a larger

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:45.160
machine on day one.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:50.560
Rod Holt replaced the heavy linear supply common in other systems with a switching supply

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:51.780
that cut weight and heat.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:57.880
Jerry Manock gave it a foam molded plastic case, a finished shell rather than an open chassis.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:04.360
Those choices were made one at a time in 1976, while Wozniak was still at Hewlett-Packard.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:09.860
Each one was checked against a single goal, unpack the machine, and use it with almost nothing

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:10.480
else to buy.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.280
A switching supply runs at higher frequency, so the transformers and capacitors can be smaller

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.960
than the ones in a linear supply.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.460
That is a real cut in copper and iron.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.540
The finished retail price still had to carry the cost of designing that supply and qualifying

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:26.800
it for safety in a home.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:33.400
The board only listing was $598, and that bought 4 kilobytes of RAM. Case,

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:39.540
keyboard, and power supply brought the complete system to $1298. Split that gap and the

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.840
leftover money is not hiding in the processor. It sits in the enclosure, the assembly, the

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.360
documentation, and integer basic already burned into ROM.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.140
Now the magic wand. Wave it, arrange the silicon, the discrete parts, and the bareboard

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:59.260
for free except for the atoms and the raw stock, and a rough estimate of that floor sits well

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:06.960
below even the board only price. Exact commodity prices for 1977 DRAM and the 6502 are not

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:12.560
in public price lists, so the floor stays a rough estimate, not a receipt. You can still see the

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:20.680
shape of the idiot index. Take the finished sticker $1,298 and set it against the board only $598.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:25.440
1298 divided by 598 lands a little above 2.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.760
Call that roughly 2-1 for the lowest configuration, and remember what you just divided.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:36.120
The denominator is already a populated board with markup, not a pile of raw silicon.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:41.600
Push the denominator down to that lower magic wand floor, and the index on the finished

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:42.880
system climbs further.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.200
A high idiot index is a signal.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:49.400
The screaming cost is integration and presentation, not the chips.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:55.120
Wazniak later wrote in bite that a personal computer should be small, reliable, convenient,

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:56.100
and inexpensive.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:02.060
The redesign went after convenience and reliability by moving functions onto the motherboard.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:06.920
The slots were a refusal to lock the buyer into one configuration at the factory.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:12.420
Memory and interfaces stayed unsoddered until the owner chose them, so the base bill of

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:17.240
material stayed lower, while the case and supply still had to support whatever came later.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:22.640
Before that machine, most microcomputers left the store as bareboards or partial kits.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:28.840
The buyer supplied the enclosure, a linear power supply, an RF modulator, and often

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:29.360
the monitor.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.220
At clubs such as the Homebrew Computer Club, people built from published schematics

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:35.820
and whatever chips they could source.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:40.460
The culture treated the computer as an ongoing project, not a finished product.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.940
Kit prices often started several hundred dollars lower because the buyer accepted the soldering

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.640
and the chance that the thing would not fully work.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:52.080
Steve Jobs had already watched that pattern on the Apple One.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.440
He wanted the next machine aimed at households and schools.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:04:00.600
The old assumption was that a serious user would tolerate exposed boards and custom wiring.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.000
That kept the paper part count down.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:07.800
It also kept the volume down because only people willing to finish the assembly would buy.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:12.760
The default path in that market was a small improvement on the last hobbyist design.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.260
Wazniak declined that limit when he laid out the new board.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:19.800
Most earlier hobby machines treated video as an add-on.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:24.460
You bought a video terminal or a display board, and you carried that cost yourself.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:29.560
Moving the video circuitry onto the main board made a television the display and took away

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.220
the terminal every buyer had previously sourced alone.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:38.160
A television still needed a small RF modulator, which Apple did not ship and owners bought

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:39.120
from third parties.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.240
The machine produced color graphics from its own circuitry, leaving those decisions to

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:45.620
the owner had lowered the sticker.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.360
It had also narrowed who would walk out with one.

00:04:49.326 --> 00:04:53.206
One's visit calc appeared, the Apple II reached schools and small businesses.

00:04:53.886 --> 00:04:57.086
Its margins stayed wider than those of later commodity machines.

00:04:57.706 --> 00:05:03.606
Volume in 1977 was still modest, so the learning that later high volume runs would bring had

00:05:03.606 --> 00:05:04.786
not shown up in the price.

00:05:04.786 --> 00:05:08.986
Further compression would have needed either those higher volumes or a different

00:05:08.986 --> 00:05:14.266
way of making cases and power supplies. Neither happened at Apple that year. The molded

00:05:14.266 --> 00:05:19.106
case and the eight slots preserved expandability and a finished look at the expense of the lowest

00:05:19.106 --> 00:05:23.066
possible price. Later clones would simplify or strip both.

00:05:23.586 --> 00:05:28.146
Foam molding added material and tooling that a simple sheet metal box would have skipped,

00:05:28.706 --> 00:05:33.486
and it also spared the buyer from fabricating a housing. The supply, more efficient than

00:05:33.486 --> 00:05:36.886
a linear one, still needed its own certification and sourcing.

00:05:37.446 --> 00:05:40.026
Work a kit builder would have done one unit at a time.

00:05:40.546 --> 00:05:45.006
Those pieces kept the gap between board cost and system price from closing in the first

00:05:45.006 --> 00:05:45.346
year.

00:05:45.926 --> 00:05:50.106
The machine worked as a product because the purchaser did not have to solve the integration.

00:05:50.666 --> 00:05:54.126
The same decisions left later competitors a clear place to attack.

00:05:54.686 --> 00:05:56.126
The enclosure and the supply.

00:05:57.132 --> 00:06:01.572
When the visible cost of a machine is the case and the integration, not the processor,

00:06:01.572 --> 00:06:04.092
The constraint you were looking at is assembly.

00:06:04.792 --> 00:06:06.752
Silicon was already a catalog item.

00:06:07.252 --> 00:06:10.292
The leap was deciding which job still deserved their own box.

00:06:10.932 --> 00:06:15.532
Treating the television as the display deleted the dedicated video terminal that hobbyist

00:06:15.532 --> 00:06:17.912
designs had kept carrying forward out of habit.

00:06:18.472 --> 00:06:21.052
That is reasoning by analogy in physical form.

00:06:21.572 --> 00:06:24.812
Last year's interface copied because last year's builder had one.

00:06:25.452 --> 00:06:30.372
First principles asks a blunter question, does this function need its own part, its own

00:06:30.372 --> 00:06:35.232
supply its own certification, or can it live on the board the buyer already paid for?

00:06:35.692 --> 00:06:39.592
The Apple II answered that for video, and only partly for power and housing.

00:06:40.112 --> 00:06:45.012
Holtz supply got closer to the material floor by shrinking magnetics, then stepped away

00:06:45.012 --> 00:06:48.132
from that floor again when safety qualification entered the price.

00:06:48.852 --> 00:06:50.872
Manox case did the same with tooling.

00:06:51.492 --> 00:06:55.272
A low idiot index would have meant the finished thing was near the cost of its atoms.

00:06:55.272 --> 00:07:01.012
A high one, which this system still had, meant the factory steps between catalog chips and

00:07:01.012 --> 00:07:02.812
a schoolroom appliance were the prize.

00:07:03.532 --> 00:07:07.092
You see the same shift whenever a platform moves from kit to appliance.

00:07:07.732 --> 00:07:11.812
Someone draws a line between what the user will assemble and what must arrive working.

00:07:12.352 --> 00:07:16.952
Everything on the wrong side of that line becomes enclosure, qualification, and software

00:07:16.952 --> 00:07:20.572
in ROM, and the sticker jumps even if the processor does not.

00:07:20.572 --> 00:07:23.372
The useful habit is to write the list twice.

00:07:23.892 --> 00:07:28.952
Once as raw parts anyone can buy, once as the finished object a non-builder will accept.

00:07:29.572 --> 00:07:33.292
The distance between those lists is the idiot index you can actually attack.

00:07:33.972 --> 00:07:37.952
What would change if the next generation of educational hardware started from the same

00:07:37.952 --> 00:07:40.872
list of catalog parts instead of from last year's case?

